#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106814} :activity/id #uuid "5616fc0b-edd6-562d-b17b-956a73aff3f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050802 :topic/id #uuid "68088430-21e6-5111-bf94-00596620d05f" :topic/math-academy-id 1331} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107037 :tutorial/content "The **power of product rule** states that if we want to raise the product $xy$ to some power $n$, we must raise each factor to the power $n$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n(xy)^n=x^n y^n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n(xy)^4=x^4y^4.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way, changing the order of factors in the multiplication. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(xy)^{4} & = \\\\ (xy)(xy)(xy)(xy) & = \\\\ (x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x)(y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "7aabbc0f-a3ca-5f05-b6ae-d7551b16ae1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "139aab34-d09f-505c-96f5-208298444874" :step/math-academy-id 3541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105441 :question/id #uuid "ac7cb5c7-c6f1-5418-92ea-025b88681439" :question/math-academy-id "e-1081"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4ce383-37b4-5d5a-80ef-3886605bd56f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051009}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553531}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Power of Product Rule with a Positi